>
  <p>A microwave and an air fryer can crowd a kitchen bench before the kettle and toaster arrive. A convection microwave with an air-fry mode offers another approach: reheating, baking, grilling and crisping in one appliance. Whether that improves your kitchen depends on the meals you cook and the space the appliance needs while operating.</p>
  <p><strong>A combination appliance may replace two machines, but it still has one cooking cavity.</strong> If you often microwave vegetables while an air fryer cooks the main course, combining those jobs can introduce a queue. If you mainly reheat lunch and occasionally bake or roast a small meal, the same trade-off may be worthwhile.</p>

  <h2>What the cooking modes actually mean</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Microwave:</strong> useful for reheating, defrosting and cooking with microwave energy. It does not provide the same dry surface browning as an oven or grill.</li>
    <li><strong>Convection:</strong> fan-assisted oven heating for suitable baking and roasting tasks. This label alone does not establish an air-fry function.</li>
    <li><strong>Grill:</strong> radiant heat for browning suitable food near the heating element, using the prescribed rack or tray.</li>
    <li><strong>Air fry:</strong> a model-specific hot-air programme and accessory arrangement. Check its temperature range, food quantities and turning instructions.</li>
    <li><strong>Combination:</strong> selected modes working together or in a programmed sequence on the same food. This is different from two independently controlled cooking compartments.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A basket air fryer offers a pull-out drawer for loading and shaking food. A microwave-style oven normally involves opening a door and handling a hot basket, rack or tray. Try those movements in a showroom if grip, reach or lifting is a concern.</p>

  <h2>Choose around a normal dinner</h2>
  <h3>One appliance may suit</h3>
  <p>You need to replace a microwave, want occasional oven or grill cooking, and usually cook those jobs one after another. You have a permanent operating position with the required ventilation and somewhere to store the unused accessories. The value comes from using several functions regularly.</p>
  <h3>Separate appliances may suit</h3>
  <p>You regularly cook two foods at once, prefer the drawer-and-shake routine, or already have an air fryer that produces results you like. A dual-drawer model can also offer separate settings for each compartment. Our <a href="/guides/small-kitchen-appliances-what-to-check-before-you-buy/">small kitchen appliance guide</a> explains equal and unequal basket layouts and the measurements to check.</p>
  <h3>Keeping what you own may suit</h3>
  <p>If both appliances work and there is enough safe operating space, replacement may solve very little. Clear the preparation area first with our <a href="/guides/kitchen-bench-space-what-to-clear-before-buying-more-storage/">bench-space guide</a>. Remember that one larger appliance can still occupy more usable bench than expected once its door and ventilation are included.</p>

  <h2>Measure tray space, not just litres</h2>
  <p>A 34-litre oven cavity and a basket air fryer's advertised capacity are different shapes. Neither figure tells you how many pieces can sit in a useful single layer. Measure your dinner plate, roasting dish and handles; check that the dish can rotate where required. Rack height and clearance above the food matter too.</p>
  <p>Check the recipe's stated food weight and arrangement. A deeper pile may need turning, shaking or several batches. Include preheating, loading, turning, standing time and washing up when comparing the two routines. We have not measured comparative cooking times or electricity consumption, so this guide makes no blanket speed or energy-saving claim.</p>

  <h2>Ventilation can decide whether it fits</h2>
  <p>The Panasonic NN-CD88QSQPQ example below measures 560 mm wide, 343 mm high and 450 mm deep before operating clearances. It weighs 18 kg, making a permanent position more practical than daily lifting. Those are appliance dimensions, not a cabinet opening specification.</p>
  <p>Its Australian manual specifies 150 mm above and 100 mm behind for counter-top placement, with a side-clearance/open-space condition that must also be followed. Do not treat those two measurements as a complete installation plan. Read the full placement instructions on page 10. Built-in installation requires Panasonic's specified trim kit and its separate instructions; a closed cupboard is not a substitute.</p>
  <p>For any model, check the open door, plug, vents and a safe landing area for hot dishes. Do not use another model's clearances or block vents with stored accessories.</p>

  <h2>Use the cookware chart for the selected mode</h2>
  <p>Microwave-safe plastic is not automatically suitable for oven heat. Likewise, a metal baking tray that works in convection mode may be prohibited when microwaves are active. Supplied metal accessories have their own mode restrictions; their inclusion does not make arbitrary metal cookware safe.</p>
  <p>For example, Panasonic's chart on page 13 permits its air-fry basket in grill and convection/air-fry modes, but not microwave or combination modes. Its supplied enamel tray and wire rack have different permissions. Check the exact chart before cooking, use appropriate oven gloves and let hot accessories cool before cleaning. Our <a href="/guides/cookware-materials-compared/">cookware materials guide</a> provides broader context, but the appliance and cookware instructions govern compatibility.</p>

